@defgroup micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cpp-api-test-case Test Cases
@brief Test cases are named sequences of checks.

@details
Each test case is defined by calling `suite::test()` with a descriptive
name and a callable object (typically a lambda accepting a `test_case&`
reference), along with any optional arguments required for the test. The
test case runs immediately, within the call. Nested test cases are
defined with `test_case::test()`, and a test case for each value of a
range or a tuple with `test_each()`.

Test cases allow related checks to be organised together within the same
environment, improving clarity and maintainability. Any number of test cases
may be defined, each focusing on a specific aspect of the code under test.

By grouping checks in this way, developers can identify which functionality
is being verified and interpret results in the context of overall software
quality.

@par Examples

@code{.cpp}
namespace mt = micro_os_plus::micro_test_plus;

// In main(), after tr.add_top_suite():
ts.test ("Check various conditions", [] (auto& t) {
  t.expect (mt::eq (compute_answer (), 42)) << "answer eq 42";
  t.expect (mt::ne (compute_answer (), 43)) << "answer ne 43";
});
@endcode

@par Test cases for several values

`test_each()`, called on a suite or on a test case, creates and runs a
test case for each value of a range (a container, an array, a span, or a
view), of a list written in place, or of a tuple, whose values may have
different types. The callable receives the test case and the value, and a
generic lambda is needed for a tuple. Each test case is named after the
common name, followed by the value in parentheses, such as `Sizes (16)`;
a value that cannot be written, such as an application type without an
`operator<<`, is replaced by its position, starting at 1, such as
`Records (#2)`.

@code{.cpp}
namespace mt = micro_os_plus::micro_test_plus;

ts.test_each ("Sizes", std::vector<int>{ 1, 16, 512 },
              [] (auto& t, int size) { t.expect (mt::gt (size, 0)); });

ts.test_each ("Limits", { 3, 4 },
              [] (auto& t, int limit) { t.expect (mt::gt (limit, 2)); });

ts.test_each ("Types", std::tuple{ 42, 1.5, "text" },
              [] (auto& t, const auto& value) {
                t.expect (mt::eq (value, value));
              });
@endcode

With the TAP reporter, the first call is reported as:

@code{.console}
    ok 1 - Sizes (1) # { passed, 1 check }
    ok 2 - Sizes (16) # { passed, 1 check }
    ok 3 - Sizes (512) # { passed, 1 check }
@endcode
